### Why do you need manual configuration for two databases?

Spring Boot's auto-configuration assumes ONE database and wires one DataSource, one EntityManagerFactory, and one TransactionManager. The moment you need two, you take manual control of all three beans for each database. The pattern is symmetric: everything you define once for the single-database case, you now define twice.

```yaml
# application.yml: two database connection configs
app:
  datasource:
    core:
      url: jdbc:postgresql://db-host:5432/core_bank
      username: core_user
      password: secret
    audit:
      url: jdbc:postgresql://audit-host:5432/audit_bank
      username: audit_user
      password: secret
```

```text
Single-database auto-config provides:
- 1 DataSource, 1 EntityManagerFactory, 1 TransactionManager

Two-database manual config provides:
- 2 DataSources, 2 EntityManagerFactories, 2 TransactionManagers
- Each wired to its own entity package and repository package
```

### How do you define the DataSource beans?

Define one DataSource per database, binding connection properties from distinct config prefixes. Mark one as @Primary.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.core")
    public DataSourceProperties coreDataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource coreDataSource() {
        return coreDataSourceProperties().initializeDataSourceBuilder().build();
    }

    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.audit")
    public DataSourceProperties auditDataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean
    public DataSource auditDataSource() {
        return auditDataSourceProperties().initializeDataSourceBuilder().build();
    }
}
```

### How do you define the EntityManagerFactory beans?

Each EntityManagerFactory scans ONLY its own entity package. This prevents Hibernate from mapping audit entities against the core database.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.boot.orm.jpa.EntityManagerFactoryBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class JpaConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean coreEntityManagerFactory(
            EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder,
            @Qualifier("coreDataSource") DataSource dataSource) {
        return builder.dataSource(dataSource)
                .packages("com.bank.core.model")       // CORE entities only
                .persistenceUnit("core")
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean auditEntityManagerFactory(
            EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder,
            @Qualifier("auditDataSource") DataSource dataSource) {
        return builder.dataSource(dataSource)
                .packages("com.bank.audit.model")      // AUDIT entities only
                .persistenceUnit("audit")
                .build();
    }
}
```

### How do you define the TransactionManager beans?

Each TransactionManager is bound to its EntityManagerFactory. A @Transactional method resolves to one of these.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import jakarta.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.JpaTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;

@Configuration
public class TransactionConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public PlatformTransactionManager coreTransactionManager(
            @Qualifier("coreEntityManagerFactory") EntityManagerFactory emf) {
        return new JpaTransactionManager(emf);
    }

    @Bean
    public PlatformTransactionManager auditTransactionManager(
            @Qualifier("auditEntityManagerFactory") EntityManagerFactory emf) {
        return new JpaTransactionManager(emf);
    }
}
```

### How do you wire repositories to each database?

Each database's repositories live in a separate package and are wired with @EnableJpaRepositories pointing at the correct factory and transaction manager.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.config.EnableJpaRepositories;

@Configuration
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.bank.core.repository",
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "coreEntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "coreTransactionManager")
class CoreJpaConfig {}

@Configuration
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.bank.audit.repository",
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "auditEntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "auditTransactionManager")
class AuditJpaConfig {}
```

```text
Package structure:
com.bank.core.model/        -> Account, Trade (core DB entities)
com.bank.core.repository/   -> AccountRepository (core DB repos)
com.bank.audit.model/       -> AuditLog (audit DB entities)
com.bank.audit.repository/  -> AuditLogRepository (audit DB repos)
```

### What about cross-database transactions?

A single @Transactional commits against ONE transaction manager, hence ONE database. If a method writes to both databases, those are two independent local transactions. If the second fails after the first commits, the two systems are inconsistent.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import com.bank.core.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.audit.repository.AuditLogRepository;
import com.bank.core.model.Account;
import com.bank.audit.model.AuditLog;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepository;
    private final AuditLogRepository auditLogRepository;

    public TransferService(AccountRepository accountRepository,
                           AuditLogRepository auditLogRepository) {
        this.accountRepository = accountRepository;
        this.auditLogRepository = auditLogRepository;
    }

    @Transactional  // commits against CORE database only (@Primary)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        Account from = accountRepository.findById(fromId).orElseThrow();
        Account to = accountRepository.findById(toId).orElseThrow();
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
        // This commits to core DB
    }

    @Transactional("auditTransactionManager")  // commits against AUDIT database
    public void writeAuditLog(AuditLog log) {
        auditLogRepository.save(log);
        // This commits to audit DB
    }
}
```

```text
Cross-database risk:
- transfer() commits to core DB
- writeAuditLog() commits to audit DB
- If writeAuditLog() fails AFTER transfer() committed -> inconsistency
- Fix: XA/JTA for atomic cross-DB, or saga/outbox for eventual consistency
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is putting both databases' entities in the same package, causing Hibernate to try mapping audit entities against the core database. A second mistake is assuming @Transactional spans both databases.

```java
// WRONG: entities in same package -> Hibernate maps ALL to one DB
com.bank.model/Account.java       // core entity
com.bank.model/AuditLog.java      // audit entity
// EntityManagerFactory scans com.bank.model -> maps BOTH to core DB!

// CORRECT: separate packages
com.bank.core.model/Account.java   // scanned by core EMF
com.bank.audit.model/AuditLog.java // scanned by audit EMF
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer disables or scopes DataSourceAutoConfiguration, configures Flyway/Liquibase per database, sizes connection pools independently, and designs for eventual consistency with a saga or outbox pattern instead of XA when possible. They explicitly specify the transaction manager on audit-writing methods.

```java
// Senior pattern: explicit transaction manager on audit writes
@Transactional("auditTransactionManager")
public void writeAuditLog(AuditLog log) {
    auditLogRepository.save(log);
}

// Outbox pattern: write audit event to core DB outbox table
// A separate process reads the outbox and writes to audit DB
// Avoids cross-DB transaction entirely
```
